      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f5e359a1_b4e4282b",
        "filename": "Tethering/src/com/android/networkstack/tethering/util/SyncStateMachine.java",
        "patchSetId": 17
      },
      "lineNbr": 40,
      "author": {
        "id": 1015428
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-09-14T07:04:06Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It should be relatively easy to do this by looking at the class of the state. In addAllStates, you could do something like\n\n    final Set\u003cClass\u003e usedClasses \u003d new ArraySet\u003c\u003e();\n    for (final StateInfo info : stateInfos) {\n      Objects.requireNonNull(info.state);\n      if (!usedClasses.add(info.state.getClass()) {\n        throw new IllegalStateException(\"duplicated state is not allowed\");\n      }\n      mStateInfo.put(info.state, info);\n    }\n\nOr you could look up the values or any other way.\n\nNow it\u0027s very easy to defeat this with the trick I was explaining above (just make an anonymous subclass). But I think there is value to prevent accidents. Maybe you just reword the error message.\n\n    \"Adding the same state multiple times in a state machine is forbidden because\n      it tends to be confusing ; it can be done with anonymous subclasses but\n      consider carefully whether you want to use a single state or other\n      alternatives instead\"",
      "parentUuid": "86b7db4a_5150b12d",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 39,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 40,
        "endChar": 15
      },
